vscode: "Add to workspace dictionary" in the lightbulb menu
open: ARCHITECTURE.md
vimrc: set spell spellfile=dojo.utf-8.add

@brief
Spell checking flags three words in this document. Two of them are
correct — `tsyringe` and `prisma` are the names of libraries, and they
will appear in every doc this project writes. Mark both as good so
they stop being flagged, top to bottom.

The third, `cancelation`, is a spelling the dictionary tolerates but
the house style does not: this project writes `cancellation`. Mark it
as wrong so it is flagged as a plain error from now on.

The document itself must not change. What you are editing is the word
list vim keeps for you, which for this lesson is `dojo.utf-8.add` in
the working directory. That file is what gets checked.

@keys
| `zg` | mark the word under the cursor as good |
| `zw` | mark it as wrong |
| `zug` `zuw` | undo either |
| `]s` | next flagged word |

@hints
`]s` finds the first flagged word. `zg` adds it to your word list.
--
`]s` `zg` twice for the two library names, then `]s` `zw` on
`cancelation`.
--
`]szg ]szg ]szw :wq`. The `:wq` is only there to end the session; the
document is unchanged and the word list was written the moment you
pressed `zg`.

@solution
    ]szg ]szg ]szw :wq<Enter>

Sixteen keystrokes.

`zg` appends the word to the file named by `spellfile` and rebuilds
the compiled list beside it, immediately. `zw` appends the word with a
`/!` suffix, which means "this is wrong even if the dictionary says
otherwise". Both take effect in every buffer from then on. Open the
`.add` file if you are curious; it is plain text, one word per line,
and safe to edit or commit.

The distinction to keep straight is `zg` versus `1z=`. `1z=` changes
the document; `zg` changes the dictionary. A flagged word that is
right — a library, a product, a surname — gets `zg`, once, and never
bothers you again. A flagged word that is wrong gets corrected.

By default the word list lives under `~/.vim/spell/`. Many people
point `spellfile` at a file inside the repo instead, so the project's
vocabulary travels with the project.

@start ARCHITECTURE.md
# How the service is wired

Every class is registered with tsyringe at startup. Providers hold the
business rules, repositories hold the data access, and services hold
anything that talks to the outside world.

The schema is owned by prisma. Migrations are immutable: a change means
a new migration, never an edit to an old one.

A cancelation outside the refund window is not an error. The order
still moves to CANCELLED and the reason is recorded.

@expected dojo.utf-8.add
tsyringe
prisma
cancelation/!
